  "debate": {
    "title":"Does rearmament make war more likely?",
    "theses":[
      {"title":"A — Military build-up can create a security dilemma","argument":"States may interpret defensive measures as offensive threats. Offensive capabilities, first-strike incentives and short decision times can fuel arms races and escalation.","evidence":"Europe before 1914 is a warning about mobilisation pressure and misperception, not proof that every arms build-up causes war.","uncertainty":"Intentions, force posture, transparency and political context matter; capability alone does not determine outcomes.","source":"jervis"},
      {"title":"B — Inadequate defence can invite aggression","argument":"An aggressor may act when it expects quick success, low cost and a weak response. Credible denial reduces the expected payoff.","evidence":"Appeasement and late rearmament did not prevent further German aggression in the 1930s. Russia attacked non-NATO Ukraine in 2022, while the reinforced NATO eastern flank has not been openly attacked.","uncertainty":"These cases are not mathematical proof; deterrence can fail and military strength can be misused.","source":"nato-deterrence"}
    ],
    "conclusion":"Not every arms build-up creates security. Credible, defensive and alliance-bound capacity can reduce an attack's chance of success. It should be combined with diplomacy, arms control, crisis communication and political predictability."
  },
  "objections": [
    {"title":"‘Better red than dead.’","core":"Avoiding nuclear war and refusing to kill are morally serious positions. A democracy must protect conscientious objection rather than treat it as disloyalty.","reply":"The dilemma does not end with refusing to defend today. If coercion destroyed Germany's democratic self-determination, there is no guarantee that tomorrow's order would still protect protest or conscientious objection; it could instead compel people to serve further expansion. Russia's present system illustrates that asymmetry: refusal and desertion can bring long prison terms, and coercive pressure to sign military contracts has been reported. This is a warning about lost rights, not a prediction that every defeat would produce exactly that outcome.","evidence":"EUAA documents compulsory Russian service, reported coercive contract signing and desertion penalties of seven to fifteen years during mobilisation or war.","uncertainty":"Nuclear escalation remains catastrophic, and the political form of any hypothetical Russian influence over Germany cannot be known. Defence, diplomacy, arms control and escalation control must therefore be pursued together.","source":"euaa-russia-service"},
    {"title":"‘Soldiers are murderers.’","core":"Tucholsky's provocation preserves an essential moral warning: military organisations prepare people for organised violence, and no uniform removes individual responsibility. Soldiers can kill unlawfully and armed forces can commit crimes.","reply":"But ‘murderer’ is not an honest blanket description of every soldier. In a parliamentary democracy soldiers do not alone decide whether the state goes to war; they carry out a legally controlled mandate while bearing its most immediate physical and moral risks. They train for the possibility of war, may have to kill and can themselves be killed or injured. Precisely because they confront these consequences directly, many may be especially cautious about war — but that cannot be claimed for every soldier. The democratic ideal is neither hero worship nor collective condemnation: it is the thinking ‘citizen in uniform’ who is bound by law and conscience and must refuse criminal orders.","evidence":"The phrase comes from Tucholsky's 1931 critique of war. In 1995 Germany's Constitutional Court overturned convictions after an inadequate free-speech analysis, but explicitly did not declare soldiers and murderers equivalent. Bundeswehr doctrine itself states that service can require risking one's life and, ultimately, killing in battle; it also rejects blind obedience.","uncertainty":"Soldiers can be victims, protectors, perpetrators or several of these across a conflict. Democratic control, international humanitarian law, training, investigation and individual accountability are what prevent military necessity from becoming a licence for crime.","sources":["bverfg-soldiers","inner-leadership"]},
    {"title":"‘If war starts, I will just leave.’","core":"Flight can be a lawful, rational and deeply understandable way to protect oneself, children or dependent relatives. Survival is not cowardice, civilians are not bargaining chips, and people who qualify for protection retain their rights regardless of whether others approve of their choices.","reply":"But ‘leave’ is not a complete contingency plan. It has at least five gates: leaving early enough and being legally permitted to exit; finding a functioning route while airspace, fuel, rail and roads are disrupted; being admitted by a destination state; obtaining a status that permits more than a short visit; and securing housing, income, healthcare and schooling for an unknown duration. Ukraine shows that martial law can restrict departure for people subject to mobilisation. Canada illustrates the destination problem: Germans normally need a passport and eTA to fly there, but an eTA does not guarantee admission; asylum requires an individual protection case, and emergency programmes are political choices that can close or change. A continent-wide NATO war could create flows far beyond existing schemes. Borders might remain open, but no individual can safely assume that a distant liberal democracy will create unlimited admission, work and settlement rights at the required moment.","evidence":"Ukraine restricted exit for most military-age men after mobilisation. Canada ended new applications to its special CUAET route in 2023 and later narrowed or time-limited follow-on measures. EU Fundamental Rights Agency evidence also shows that legal protection does not guarantee a comfortable life: surveyed displaced Ukrainian women reported financial hardship, unsafe accommodation, exploitation and violence. In 2026 the United States did deport Ukrainian men under immigration enforcement; reporting found that some were transferred to Ukrainian recruitment offices after arrival. That was not a blanket expulsion of Ukrainians solely because they were of draft age.","uncertainty":"A Germany–NATO war is unprecedented, so neither sealed borders nor generous admission can be predicted. Canada or other allies might create new protection routes. Article 5 obliges each ally to assist but lets it choose the measures it considers necessary; it does not promise that named Canadian soldiers will replace every German who leaves. The burden-sharing tension is politically real, yet refugee protection must not be made conditional on military service. The responsible conclusion is narrower: flight may be necessary, but it should not be romanticised as guaranteed, easy or permanent; documents, family meeting points, official evacuation information, nearby shelter options and a lawful contribution to civil resilience are more credible preparation than a single distant destination.","sources":["unhcr-refugee-convention","ohchr-ukraine-exit","canada-entry","canada-ukraine-measures","fra-ukraine-flight","cnn-us-ukraine-removals","nato-article5"]},
    {"title":"‘Russia has no interest in Germany.’","core":"A deliberate invasion of Germany is not the most direct or necessary path for Russian goals.","reply":"In this stress chain Germany matters as a NATO decision-maker and logistics hub; disruption could serve coercive goals without territorial conquest.","evidence":"German and NATO planning explicitly treats host-nation support and reinforcement as strategic functions.","uncertainty":"Intentions can change and cannot be read with certainty.","source":"op-de"},
    {"title":"‘NATO threatens Russia.’","core":"Russian leaders may perceive enlargement and Allied forces as threatening.","reply":"Perception matters, but it does not erase the sovereignty of neighbouring states or justify aggression. NATO defines its posture as defensive and decisions are taken by member governments.","evidence":"The North Atlantic Treaty and current NATO posture frame collective defence, not conquest.","uncertainty":"Security dilemmas persist even when declared intentions are defensive.","source":"nato-treaty"},
    {"title":"‘More weapons automatically mean more war.’","core":"Weapons can intensify arms races and make escalation more destructive.","reply":"The effect depends on posture and incentives. Defensive denial, transparency and alliance control can reduce expected gains from aggression.","evidence":"Security-dilemma research distinguishes offensive advantage from distinguishable defensive posture.","uncertainty":"No force structure is interpreted identically by every actor.","source":"jervis"},
    {"title":"‘In nuclear war, defence is pointless.’","core":"Large-scale nuclear war would be catastrophic and civil protection cannot make it controllable.","reply":"Defence also aims to prevent conventional coercion and keep escalation below nuclear thresholds. Preparedness can still save lives in outages and conventional emergencies.","evidence":"NATO combines deterrence with resilience; BBK advice covers multiple crises without promising total protection.","uncertainty":"Nuclear escalation remains a residual existential risk.","source":"bbk"},
    {"title":"‘Diplomacy is the only solution.’","core":"Wars end politically; communication and negotiated limits are indispensable.","reply":"Diplomacy works under incentives and constraints. If one side expects force to deliver more, talks alone may not prevent aggression.","evidence":"Arms control was strongest when paired with verification, deterrence and sustained communication.","uncertainty":"The right mix changes with timing and counterpart.","source":"osce"},
    {"title":"‘Professional armies are enough.’","core":"Modern forces need skilled, long-serving professionals.","reply":"Protracted defence also needs reserve, logistics, medical, cyber, infrastructure and host-nation capacity. Professionals remain the core, but scale may require trained augmentation.","evidence":"German defence planning assigns roles to active forces, reserve, territorial defence and civilian actors.","uncertainty":"Actual personnel need depends on strategy, technology and alliance burden-sharing.","source":"op-de"},
    {"title":"‘Conscription is inherently undemocratic.’","core":"Compulsory service is a serious intrusion into freedom and can be unfair.","reply":"Democratic legitimacy depends on law, necessity, equality, conscientious objection, oversight, fair compensation and real capacity to train people safely.","evidence":"Germany's Basic Law protects conscientious objection and contains a legal framework for compulsory service.","uncertainty":"A lawful model can still be disproportionate or poorly designed.","source":"basic-law"},
    {"title":"‘Germany could simply remain neutral.’","core":"Neutrality can reduce alliance entanglement in some geopolitical settings.","reply":"Germany is geographically and economically embedded in the EU and NATO. Unilateral neutrality would not erase infrastructure, cyber or coercion risks and would require political and treaty choices with major costs.","evidence":"Germany has binding alliance commitments and sits at the centre of European logistics and markets.","uncertainty":"Neutrality outcomes depend on recognition by others and credible self-defence.","source":"nato-treaty"}
