  "people": {
    "title":"Why defence needs people",
    "roles":["Active force for ready operations and specialised capability","Reserve for reinforcement, endurance and replacement","Territorial defence and protection of defence-relevant functions","Host-nation support and logistics for Allied movement","Medical services and casualty care","Cyber and secure communications","Civil protection and continuity of essential services","Training before a crisis, because skills and units cannot be improvised"],
    "serviceDebate": {
      "title":"Service to country and democratic community — which model serves which purpose?",
      "intro":"Service should mean responsibility for the democratic community and for the people who live in it — not obedience to a government or to an ethnic idea of ‘the people’. Military readiness, civil resilience and social learning are legitimate but different goals. A single compulsory programme cannot be assumed to achieve all three.",
      "learning":"Well-designed service can create contact across social groups, practical responsibility, teamwork, conflict skills, self-reliance and occupational orientation. German evaluations of voluntary services and the former civilian service found such development. The evidence does not prove that compulsion itself causes it: self-selection, placement quality, supervision and meaningful work matter. A democratic state may enable learning, but should not claim a mandate to discipline young adults into political conformity.",
      "models":[
        {"title":"Classic German model: military duty plus conscientious-objector service","scope":"Under the traditional Article 12a structure, men can be called for military service; recognised conscientious objectors perform civilian substitute service. It is not an equal duty for every gender.","purpose":"Primarily military personnel and reserve generation; the civilian track protects freedom of conscience and can add capacity in social institutions.","benefit":"More predictable military intake, a trained reserve and practical service experience. Former civilian service could foster social and gender competence and expose participants to care work and lives outside their usual milieu.","risk":"Unequal treatment by gender, arbitrary cohort selection, delayed education and work, pressure to justify conscience, and the temptation to use low-paid compelled workers as a structural substitute for qualified staff. Without instructors, equipment and useful placements, duty produces waiting, frustration and little security.","law":"Reactivating the constitutional military framework is legally closer than inventing a universal civic duty, but fairness, proportionality, selection and operational capacity still require a new political and legal design.","judgement":"Better aligned with a defined military personnel gap, but socially unequal in its classic form and not a general programme for civic education."},
        {"title":"Universal civic year: military, civil-protection or social option for all genders","scope":"Every young adult would perform a time-limited service, with genuine choice among armed service, civil protection, rescue, health and care, social, environmental or other accredited public-interest placements.","purpose":"Share civic responsibility more equally, strengthen military and civilian resilience, create social encounters and offer learning and occupational orientation.","benefit":"Gender-fair participation, a broader civil-resilience pool and potentially more contact across class, region and background. Meaningful work plus pedagogical support can strengthen teamwork, empathy, confidence and social competence.","risk":"A major intrusion into liberty and life planning; hundreds of thousands of useful, supervised and additional placements would be needed each year. Exemptions and sanctions can reproduce inequality, unwilling participants can burden institutions, and compulsory labour can displace regular jobs or conceal underfunding in care. The military receives no reliable number unless enough people choose it.","law":"According to the Bundestag's legal review, prevailing opinion requires a constitutional amendment; prohibitions on forced labour under European and international law remain a high hurdle, especially for a twelve-month general duty.","judgement":"Broader and more equal in aspiration, but legally and organisationally much harder. It is a separate social-policy project, not a quick solution to an immediate defence shortfall."}
      ],
      "enforcementTitle":"The implementation test — where an attractive idea can fail",
      "enforcement":["Define a concrete public need and duration instead of using ‘character building’ as a vague justification.","Offer genuinely meaningful choices, conscientious objection, hardship exemptions and accessible judicial review.","Apply the duty across genders and social groups; otherwise selection becomes a lottery that rewards influence and penalises those with fewer resources.","Pay enough to secure independence, pension and social-insurance protection; protect training, university places and employment.","Finance qualified supervision, safeguarding, accommodation and equipment before calling a cohort.","Use compulsory personnel only in additional, educational roles — never as cheap replacement for qualified care, rescue or military professionals.","Make sanctions proportionate. Harsh enforcement may create more injustice and alienation than civic solidarity.","Evaluate competence, public benefit, later volunteering and distributional effects against a voluntary-service comparison group."],
      "conclusion":"My judgement: expand attractive voluntary service immediately and build the instructors, placements and reserve structures that any model needs. If a verified military gap remains, a needs-based, gender-equal defence service with a real civilian alternative is easier to justify than a universal year sold mainly as moral education. A universal civic year deserves its own constitutional and social debate.",
      "sources":["bundestag-general-duty","bmfsfj-voluntary-evaluation","bmfsfj-civilian-socialisation","basic-law"]
