    "refusalParadox": {
      "title":"The asymmetry of refusal: will tomorrow's ruler preserve today's right to say no?",
      "text":"A free society gives people space to oppose war, demonstrate and refuse armed service. An expansionist authoritarian order may not reciprocate that restraint. The uncomfortable counterfactual is therefore whether refusing every contribution to democratic self-defence today could help create conditions in which the same person is later denied protest and compelled to serve somebody else's expansion. That possibility strengthens the case for protecting democratic defensive capacity; it does not abolish conscientious objection or prove that military escalation is wise.",
      "evidence":"This concern is not purely abstract: the UN documented Russian conscription imposed in occupied Crimea and obstruction of applications for alternative civilian service. Current EUAA reporting describes reported pressure on Russian conscripts to sign contracts and prison exposure for refusal or desertion.",
      "limit":"This is a risk mechanism, not a forecast for Germany. It must not be used to stigmatise pacifists, Russians as a people or every opponent of conscription. The democratic answer is defence capacity plus an enforceable right to conscience — not coercion without limits.",
      "sources":["ohchr-crimea-conscription","euaa-russia-service"]
